# Operations-Analyst Brief — Quiet-by-Design at MWAA

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Author lens:** Airport operations analyst (throughput, IROPS, employee workload, passenger flow, missed-boarding rates, terminal acoustics as an operating variable rather than a branding claim)
**Date:** 2026-08-06
**Independence note:** Written without reference to any other Stage 1 brief.

---

## Executive summary

The operational literature on "quiet" or "silent" airports is thinner than the marketing literature suggests. Peer implementations at London City (2008), Helsinki (2015), Schiphol, Copenhagen, Barcelona-El Prat, Changi (2017), and Chennai (2017) are well documented as *policy decisions* but poorly documented as *measured operations*. Almost none of the public sources report before/after numbers on missed-boarding rates, gate-hold minutes, IROPS message latency, employee cognitive load, or the retail-conversion effect that the trade press asserts. This is the first operational fact MWAA needs to internalize before writing a standard: it will be writing partly against a data vacuum, and it should therefore write the standard in a way that generates the missing data going forward.

What *is* well established is the technical floor. NFPA 72 sets a Speech Transmission Index (STI) of 0.50 as the acceptance criterion for emergency voice/alarm communication in a terminal, and 0.70 as the preferred intelligibility target [Source: https://www.securitysales.com/sponsored/mass-notification-intelligibility/93663/]. ACRP Research Report 175 — the definitive US airport-terminal PA study — measured average daytime ambient noise of 61–62 dBA across the terminals it sampled, with spaces ranging from 60 to over 90 dBA, and identified reverberation time above 1.0 second as the principal barrier to speech intelligibility [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5]. Signal-to-noise ratios of 10–15 dB are the working industry design target for intelligibility [Source: https://www.pureresonanceaudio.com/products/paging-system-horn-speakers-4-zone-mixer-power-amp-paging-mic]. These are engineering facts. They are the correct starting point for a mandatory MWAA standard, and they are quiet-neutral: they hold whether MWAA reduces PA volume or expands it.

Where the standard is harder — and where the operational lens is most useful — is in the second-order effects. "Silent" is not a uniform state. Every peer that markets itself as a silent airport still runs full announcements at gates, for IROPS, for lost children, and for TSA-mandated content. The operational risk of a poorly written MWAA standard is not that it eliminates announcements; it is that it degrades the *fallback channels* airports rely on when normal operations break — during winter storms, ATC ground stops, gate reassignments, medical events, and evacuations. Concourse E at IAD, opening in autumn 2026, is the first live test of any decision MWAA makes here, and it is a hub concourse for United, where connecting passengers cannot be assumed to know their gates.

The recommendation the operations lens supports is a *tiered* standard whose mandatory tier is acoustic and technological — STI, reverberation time, ambient noise ceiling, zoned PA architecture, ambient-noise-sensing gain, redundant visual and mobile channels — and whose *announcement policy* tier is discretionary and terminal-specific, governed by measured passenger-flow data (missed-boarding rate, gate-connection makings, IROPS communication latency) rather than by a bumper-sticker rule.

