## Key findings

1. **The intelligibility floor is codified; the "quietness" ceiling is not.** NFPA 72 requires STI ≥ 0.50 in required areas with a preferred target of 0.70 [Source: https://www.securitysales.com/sponsored/mass-notification-intelligibility/93663/]. No US regulation prescribes a *maximum* announcement frequency, sound-pressure level, or spillover threshold. The mandatory tier of any MWAA standard therefore has more room upward on quality than downward on quantity.

2. **US terminal ambient noise averages 61–62 dBA, with a long right tail.** ACRP 175's field measurements across "adequate" spaces (those where STI ≥ 0.50 was achievable) averaged 61 dBA daytime ambient, but the observed range across all measured spaces was 60 to over 90 dBA [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5]. A single ambient-noise ceiling written into an MWAA standard will therefore need to be zone-specific: FIS halls, curbside vestibules, and food-court adjacencies will not hold the same number as a boarding lounge.

3. **Reverberation above 1.0 second is the principal architectural obstacle.** ACRP 175 recommends that for reverberation times of 1.1–1.5 seconds, spaces under 10,000 sq ft need acoustic treatment on 15–35% of surface area, and larger spaces need 6–28% [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5]. Both the IAD Saarinen main terminal and the Pelli-designed DCA Terminal 2 National Hall have high-volume vaulted geometry that likely puts them above this threshold; retrofit to modern STI targets is architecturally constrained and, at IAD, subject to National Historic Landmark review.

4. **Peer "silent" implementations are policy-documented but outcome-thin.** London City (2008), Helsinki (2015), Changi (2017), Chennai (2017), Copenhagen, and Schiphol have publicly stated silent-airport policies, but with the exception of a Helsinki passenger-perception exercise referenced in the trade press, none publish measured before/after operational data (missed boardings, gate-hold minutes, employee cognitive load, retail-conversion, complaint rate) [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. This is the biggest evidence gap in the whole discourse.

5. **No peer is actually silent — they are zoned.** Every publicly documented silent-airport policy retains announcements at gate areas, for IROPS, for lost children, and for emergencies [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. Helsinki removed only *flight and passenger announcements in the terminal common areas*; gate announcements and emergency messages continued [Source: https://morepremium.com/helsinki-vantaa-to-be-made-a-quiet-airport/]. The MWAA standard should therefore be a *zoning* standard, not a policy of silence.

6. **Missed-boarding risk is the operational counter-argument, and hub geometry sharpens it.** Reported experience at SFO — a large hub, roughly comparable in connection intensity to IAD — indicates passenger frustration and increased missed-boarding risk when PA was suppressed, particularly for connecting passengers arriving without local time to check FIDS [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]. IAD is a United hub with strong connecting flows to international departures; DCA is dominantly origin-and-destination. That difference argues against a single MWAA-wide announcement rule.

7. **Sensory rooms are ubiquitous at US peers but solve a different problem.** PIT operates a ~1,500 sq ft sensory room; ATL provides a sensory room in the F Concourse departure area; PHX offers a sensory space inside its Terminal 4 chapel [Source: https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2019/08/08/746936601/]. These facilities address individual episodes of overstimulation; they do not substitute for terminal-wide acoustic and PA governance. Any MWAA quiet-by-design standard should specify both (baseline acoustic quality *and* discrete low-stimulation spaces) — they are complements, not substitutes.

8. **Zoned PA, ambient-noise-sensing gain, and directional loudspeakers are the mature technology; the "quiet" outcome is a byproduct.** Industry practice for airport paging already includes zoning by process area (curb, checkpoint, hold room, baggage claim), dynamic gain that raises volume 15 dB above measured ambient during peaks and lowers it during troughs, and directional emitters that constrain spillover into adjacent zones [Source: https://blog.atlasied.com/what-is-a-quiet-airport-how-smart-technology-improves-passenger-experience]. Adopting these as MWAA mandatory technical requirements produces the quiet outcome without needing to declare a policy of silence.

