## Evidence

### 1. The technical floor: what any MWAA standard must clear

NFPA 72 (National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code), adopted through local building codes at both Arlington County (DCA) and Fairfax/Loudoun (IAD), governs emergency voice/alarm communication systems (EVACS). Its intelligibility test uses the Speech Transmission Index, an ISO/IEC 60268-16 measurement producing a 0.00–1.00 scale. NFPA 72 sets 0.50 STI as the code acceptance criterion, with values as low as 0.45 permitted if the space-average holds at 0.50; 0.70 is the preferred design target [Source: https://www.securitysales.com/sponsored/mass-notification-intelligibility/93663/]. Peer-reviewed acoustics research on pier-style departure lounges recommends SNR ≥ 10 dBA and STI ≥ 0.45 to keep announcements clearly audible while avoiding excess loudness [Source: https://doi.org/10.3390/buildings15030426].

ACRP Report 175 (Improving Intelligibility of Airport Terminal Public Address Systems, 2017) is the authoritative US airport study. Its field measurements found average daytime ambient of 61 dBA in "adequate" spaces (those meeting STI ≥ 0.50) and 62 dBA across all sampled spaces, with individual space measurements ranging from 60 to over 90 dBA. Reverberation times above 1.0 second were identified as the principal architectural obstacle to intelligibility; for reverberation of 1.1–1.5 seconds, spaces under 10,000 sq ft require acoustic treatment of 15–35% of surface area, and spaces over 10,000 sq ft require 6–28% [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5]. Industry design practice targets a 10–15 dB signal-to-noise ratio [Source: https://www.pureresonanceaudio.com/products/paging-system-horn-speakers-4-zone-mixer-power-amp-paging-mic].

**Operational implication:** The mandatory tier of an MWAA standard should specify STI ≥ 0.60 in normal-operations conditions in hold rooms and along processing paths (a target above NFPA 72's emergency-only floor), a reverberation-time ceiling of 1.0 second in occupied spaces above 5,000 sq ft, and an ambient-noise ceiling scaled by zone type. These are architecturally enforceable numbers; unlike "reduce announcements," they can be tested at commissioning and re-verified annually.

### 2. What the peer airports actually did — and what they measured

- **London City Airport (LCY), 2008:** First widely cited "silent terminal" policy. Only essential announcements are delivered via PA; gate-change and disruption announcements are retained. Wayfinding shifted to information screens throughout lounge and restaurant areas, with real-time flight information supplied via Facebook Messenger and Twitter [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. Public data on missed boardings, complaint rates, or gate-hold minutes has not been published in the sources reviewed. LCY handled ~5.9M pax/yr pre-pandemic, is a business-heavy, single-terminal, short-haul airport — an operational profile fundamentally different from IAD.

- **Helsinki-Vantaa (HEL), June 2015:** Finavia's "Silent Airport" project — jointly with airlines — eliminated flight and passenger announcements in terminal common areas while retaining gate announcements and emergency announcements. Passengers were directed to flight displays, airport website, and mobile app [Source: https://morepremium.com/helsinki-vantaa-to-be-made-a-quiet-airport/]. Trade press references a 2016 passenger-perception exercise that reported reduced stress, improved ambience, and neutral wayfinding results [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over], but the primary study is not publicly available in the sources reviewed — a genuine evidence gap.

- **Amsterdam Schiphol (AMS):** Publicly positions itself as a silent airport with "virtually no announcements," relying on FIDS and app-based notifications [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]. The airport publishes passenger satisfaction trend data but has not, in the sources reviewed, isolated the contribution of PA reduction from other quality investments.

- **Copenhagen (CPH):** Voice announcements minimized to reduce passenger stress; passengers are advised to monitor flight boards themselves [Source: https://www.airportinformation.com/CPH]. No operational-outcome data cited in sources reviewed.

- **Changi (SIN), December 2017:** Reduced announcements; public reaction on social media was mixed, with vulnerable-traveler concerns publicly voiced [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. Given SIN's connecting-traffic intensity, this reaction is directly relevant to IAD.

- **Chennai (MAA), May 2017:** Eliminated boarding announcements in favor of SMS notifications from airlines with limited PA use for gate changes [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. No comparative missed-boarding data available.

- **Dubai (DXB):** Announcements limited to emergency situations such as lost and found children [Source: https://www.sittig-tech.com/post/the-silent-airport-guide-unveiling-the-path-to-peaceful-passenger-experiences]. DXB relies heavily on staff-mediated communication and its app.

**Operational implication:** The peer set is dominated by non-US airports operating under different regulatory regimes (no US Part 139, no US TSA layered messaging, no US CBP FIS-hall communication requirements). Every peer preserved gate announcements and emergency channels. A "MWAA silent airport" claim modeled on this peer set is defensible only if it is scoped identically — terminal common areas quieted, gates and IROPS and emergencies preserved — and if MWAA generates the operational data the peers largely did not.

### 3. The operational counter-argument: missed boardings, IROPS, and the hub problem

The most operationally serious critique of silent-airport policies comes from hub geometry. Simple Flying's 2023 reporting on SFO indicated passenger frustration and elevated missed-boarding risk after PA reduction, particularly among connecting passengers who arrive at the terminal without local time to check FIDS or install an app [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]. Airport analytics vendors have responded with "dynamic call-to-gate" tools that use passenger data to identify at-risk passengers and target announcements at only those individuals [Source: https://www.paconsulting.com/insights/why-airports-must-embed-ai-into-infrastructure-to-create-a-tailwind-for-growth]. The operational point is that silent-airport policies imported without this compensating infrastructure will produce measurable missed-boarding cost.

IROPS communication is the second operational fault line. During ATC ground stops, weather diversions, gate reassignments, and medical events, the PA system is often the only channel that reaches all passengers in a zone simultaneously and in multiple languages. Airline-app push notifications reach only that airline's customers; FIDS updates lag decision-making by minutes; staff-mediated communication is slow. A quiet-by-design standard that degrades PA capability during IROPS would degrade operational recovery time and, in a Part 139 airport, would sit uncomfortably against the § 139.325 requirement for "a communications network" capable of prompt notification in the Airport Emergency Plan.

### 4. Accessibility as an operational — not just moral — constraint

The DOT ADA rule for airports (49 CFR Part 27), the ADA/ABA Accessibility Standards, and 14 CFR Part 382 all require *effective communication*, which in practice means audible information must have visual counterparts and vice versa. A silent-airport policy without redundant visual channels for hearing-impaired passengers is legally exposed; a visual-only policy without audio backup for low-vision or non-English-reading passengers is equally exposed. Sensory rooms exist at PIT (1,500 sq ft), ATL (F Concourse), and PHX (Terminal 4 chapel) [Source: https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2019/08/08/746936601/], and address individual episodes, not terminal-wide governance. The operational lesson is that the MWAA standard must specify *redundant channels* — audio, visual, digital, and staff-mediated — as a mandatory requirement, and specify the response time for each channel during IROPS.

### 5. What the technology actually delivers

Zoned PA architecture (separate audio zones for curb, checkpoint queue, retail concourse, hold room, baggage claim), directional loudspeakers, and ambient-noise-sensing dynamic gain are commercially mature technologies routinely specified in modern terminal builds [Source: https://blog.atlasied.com/what-is-a-quiet-airport-how-smart-technology-improves-passenger-experience]. The Chicago Department of Aviation's public "Common Exhibit 10 – Public Address System" specification is a working example of a large US operator writing this into procurement [Source: https://www.flychicago.com/business/opportunities/build/Documents/Common%20Exhibit%20(CE)-10%20Public%20Address%20System.pdf]. The point for MWAA is that the *technology* delivers the quiet outcome without requiring a headline "silent airport" policy — and technology mandates are easier to write into a Design Manual, easier to test at commissioning, and easier to defend to airline signatories in the U&L than announcement-policy mandates are.

