## Operational recommendation the Council can carry forward

The evidence supports a three-tier standard, structured this way:

1. **Mandatory technical tier (both airports, all new and substantially renovated spaces):** STI ≥ 0.60 in normal operations in occupied processing zones; NFPA 72's ≥ 0.50 in required emergency areas; reverberation time ≤ 1.0 sec in spaces > 5,000 sq ft; ambient noise ceilings scaled by zone type against ACRP 175 baselines; zoned PA architecture with directional emitters and ambient-noise-sensing dynamic gain; redundant visual, digital, and staff-mediated channels specified at the design stage with response-time requirements.

2. **Recommended operational tier (default policy, terminal-specific carve-outs):** Terminal common areas run quiet; gate announcements preserved; IROPS communication preserved; TSA and CBP required communication preserved; low-stimulation spaces specified for every concourse.

3. **Context-dependent tier:** IAD hub concourses (C, D replacement, E) may require higher announcement floors than DCA origin-and-destination concourses because of international connecting flows; historic-terminal envelopes (IAD main, DCA Terminal 1) require architectural acoustic intervention before any policy tier is meaningful; commercial-tenant systems (televisions, retail audio) must be governed by the same standard as MWAA-operated PA.

The mandatory tier is the operationally defensible and the airline-negotiable core. The policy tier should be run as a *measured pilot* — start at Concourse E, publish the missed-boarding, gate-hold, IROPS-message-latency, and complaint numbers, then decide.

That is the operational discipline this peer set has generally failed to impose on itself. MWAA can do better because it can write the measurement requirements into the standard at inception. That, and not the word "silent," is the real product.
